---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="time-converter">
  <UnitConverter client:load dimension="time" from="min" to="h" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a time interval</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type a duration into either field. The time converter converts lengths of time, such as 90 minutes into hours or 3 days into seconds. It does not convert clock times or dates.</li>
      <li>Pick the units: sub-second units for performance work first, then everyday units, then calendar units.</li>
      <li>For the gap between two dates, or a deadline, use the date-difference calculator. Calendar arithmetic must handle month lengths and daylight saving, which a unit converter cannot.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Exact units and averaged units</h2>
    <p>
      All units from nanoseconds to weeks are exact. A minute is 60 seconds, an hour 3,600, a day 86,400 and a
      week 604,800. Leap seconds sometimes make a real day one second longer, but ordinary calculations, and
      this one, ignore them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Months and years are calendar units, not fixed intervals. This converter uses the long-run Gregorian
      averages:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Unit</th><th>Days</th><th>Seconds</th><th>Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Year</td><td>365.2425</td><td>31,556,952</td><td>Leap years every 4 years, skipping 3 in every 400</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Month</td><td>30.436875</td><td>2,629,746</td><td>Exactly one twelfth of that year</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Week</td><td>7</td><td>604,800</td><td>Exact</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Day</td><td>1</td><td>86,400</td><td>Exact by convention</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      So "3 months" here means 91.3 days, not "the next three calendar months", which can be 89 to 92 days
      depending on the start date. For contracts, notice periods and billing cycles, count the actual dates.
    </p>

    <h2>Common time conversions</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Duration</th><th>In minutes</th><th>In hours</th><th>In seconds</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>45 minutes</td><td>45</td><td>0.75</td><td>2,700</td></tr>
          <tr><td>90 minutes</td><td>90</td><td>1.5</td><td>5,400</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 day</td><td>1,440</td><td>24</td><td>86,400</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 week</td><td>10,080</td><td>168</td><td>604,800</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 working month (160 h)</td><td>9,600</td><td>160</td><td>576,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1 year</td><td>525,949</td><td>8,765.8</td><td>31,556,952</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Sub-second units and why they matter</h2>
    <p>
      Performance work happens below the second. A millisecond is a thousandth of a second, a microsecond a
      millionth and a nanosecond a billionth. For scale: a modern SSD answers a read request in about 100
      microseconds, main memory in about 100 nanoseconds, and one CPU cycle at 3 GHz takes about a third of a
      nanosecond. Network round trips across an ocean take tens of milliseconds: about 150 ms from London to
      Sydney, a minimum set by the speed of light in fibre, not by equipment.
    </p>
    <p>
      In these units the numbers get long quickly, so the formatting keeps twelve significant digits and
      changes to scientific notation only above 10¹⁸.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
